The graph shows a curve $y = f(x)$ with two lines $L_1$ and $L_2$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] plot[smooth,tension=0.6] coordinates {(1,0.8) (2,1.8) (3,3.2) (4,4.8)} node[pos=0.5, above left] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[thick,dashed,red] (1,0.8) -- (4,4.8) node[below right] {$L_1$};
\draw[thick,teal] (1.4,0.8) -- (4.2,5.0) node[above] {$L_2$};
\fill (3,3.2) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$P(3,f(3))$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Which line's gradient represents the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x = 3$?

The graph of $y = f(x)$ has marked points $P, Q, R$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] plot[smooth,tension=0.6] coordinates {(0.5,1.0) (2,4.0) (3.5,4.0) (5,1.0)} node[above right] {$y=f(x)$};
\fill (1.0,2.2) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$P$};
\fill (2.75,4.2) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$Q$};
\fill (4.5,2.2) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
At which point is the derivative $f'(x) < 0$?
